LeetCode 100 - Same Tree
Problem
Given the roots of two binary trees, p and q, determine whether they are exactly the same.
Two trees are considered the same when:
They have the same structure.
Corresponding nodes have the same values.
Example:
Tree p:        Tree q:

    1              1
   / \            / \
  2   3          2   3
Output:
true
But these are not the same:
Tree p:        Tree q:

    1              1
   /                \
  2                  2
Even though the values are the same, the structure is different.
Output:
false


⸻


Initial Idea
My idea was to compare the two trees recursively.
For every pair of nodes:
Check whether both nodes are null.
If both nodes exist, compare them.
Recursively compare the left subtrees.
Recursively compare the right subtrees.
If only one node is null, the trees are different.
The recursive approach fits naturally because each tree can be broken down into smaller left and right subtrees.


⸻


Mistake I Made
At first, I used:
p == q
to compare two nodes.
This does not compare the values inside the nodes.
In Java, when p and q are objects:
p == q
checks whether both references point to the exact same object in memory.
For example:
p → TreeNode(val = 1)
q → TreeNode(val = 1)
Even though:
p.val == q.val
is:
true
the expression:
p == q
can still be:
false
because they are two different TreeNode objects.
The correct comparison is:
p.val == q.val


⸻


Final Approach
There are three main cases.
Case 1: Both Nodes Are Null
if (p == null && q == null)
    return true;
If both nodes are null, this part of both trees has the same structure.


⸻


Case 2: Both Nodes Exist
If both nodes are not null, three conditions must all be true:
current values are equal
AND
left subtrees are equal
AND
right subtrees are equal
So:
return p.val == q.val
        && isSameTree(p.left, q.left)
        && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);


⸻


Case 3: Only One Node Is Null
If execution reaches the end, then one node exists while the other is null.
For example:
p:          q:

    1           1
   /             \
  2               2
At some point we compare:
p.left = 2
q.left = null
So the structures are different and we return:
false


⸻


Solution
public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {

    if (p == null && q == null)
        return true;

    if (p != null && q != null)
        return p.val == q.val
                && isSameTree(p.left, q.left)
                && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);

    return false;
}


⸻


Recursive Trace
Consider:
p:              q:

     1               1
    / \             / \
   2   3           2   3
The recursive calls look like:
isSameTree(1, 1)
│
├── 1 == 1 ✅
│
├── isSameTree(2, 2)
│   │
│   ├── 2 == 2 ✅
│   ├── isSameTree(null, null) → true
│   └── isSameTree(null, null) → true
│
└── isSameTree(3, 3)
    │
    ├── 3 == 3 ✅
    ├── isSameTree(null, null) → true
    └── isSameTree(null, null) → true

Result → true
The recursion only returns true if every corresponding pair of nodes matches.


⸻


What I Learned
Recursion is a natural way to compare two binary trees.
Two trees must have both the same values and the same structure.
p == q compares object references, not the node values.
To compare the values stored inside two nodes, use:
p.val == q.val
Base cases are very important in recursive tree problems.
Comparing null positions is also necessary because tree structure matters.


⸻


Complexity
Let n be the number of nodes in the trees.
Time Complexity
O(n)
In the worst case, we visit every node once.
Space Complexity
O(h)
where h is the height of the tree.
This space is used by the recursive call stack.
For a balanced tree:
O(log n)
For a completely skewed tree:
O(n)
